                                                .post-message(data-role='message', dir='auto')
                                                    div
                                                        p
                                                            | The observation concerning “hybridization of the German shepherd dog with the Peruvian hairless dog and the xoloitzcuintli … is unexpected …” should involve consideration of the research of Raul Valadez Azua and his colleagues on Mesoamerican dogs. I realize that you cite Dr. Valadez in his collaborations with Castroviejo-Fisher et al. and Leonard et al., but many of his studies deserve further analysis in that he has noted that the hairless types received so much attention in early accounts, which continues in the recognition of kennel clubs, that it has often been overlooked that the most common Mesoamerican type had abundant hair.  (See “Estudio del perro mesoamericano,” AMMVEPE 7(3), 111-117 (1996), particularly Fig. 12, postulating the relationship between the Itzuintli, Xoloitzcuintli, Tlalchichi, and Chihuahua.) In a 2003 paper (“La investigacion etnozoologica y el studio del canido mesoamericano,” AMMVEPE 14(6), 186-194), Valadez’s team notes the widespread distribution of what he calls the common Mesoamerican dog, compared to the other types that were developed from the common type and originally concentrated around population centers of the major pre-Columbian civilizations. Cordy-Collins (“An Unshaggy Dog Story, Natural History 103(2), 34-41 (1994)) has suggested that the hairless dogs, valued as food, were brought by Ecuadorean sea traders from Mexico to South America about the 8th century AD.  It would seem to me more likely that interbreeding of imported sheep dogs would have been with the larger and more common types present in village dog populations than directly with more isolated and specialized Mexican and Peruvian types.
