                                                    div
                                                        p
                                                            | Thank you all for your past and present work. I'm interested in the development of the sighthound as a hunting breed, gratified that as North Americans you courageously posited a definition. My question is: there seems to be a parallel development/selection in sighthounds, if we split them into Eastern and Western sighthounds, or is it possible you can genetically locate them somewhere in the very far past, possibly in Eurasia, in some ur-sighthound form? Literature has it in historical time that the Galgo was crossed to the Sloughi during the Caliphate, and in more modern times with the Greyhound, it is possibly the oldest representative in the Western family of sighthounds - unfortunately I missed it in your selection of breeds, but was surprised to see a new 'breed' to me, the Levriero Meridionale, which seems to share similar characteristics of  a cross between African and European breeds. I hope we can see the Galgo in your future work and wish you success.
