                                            .publisher-anchor-color(data-role='message-content')
                                                .post-message(data-role='message', dir='auto')
                                                    div
                                                        p
                                                            | I am surprised that the discussion makes no mention of van Asch et al. 2013 (“Pre-Columbian origins of Native American dog breeds, with only limited replacement by European dogs, confirmed by mtDNA analysis”), given that that paper, though mitochondrial, is clearly in the same line of research and discusses the xolo, the perro sin pelo del Peru, the Chihuahua, the Alaskan Malamute, and also considers the survival of pre-Columbian lineages.  Is the Carolina dog one that will be added to the research? (I realize Caroline Coile already asked about this.) There are many pre-Columbian artifacts with canine DNA (breach cloths, quivers) but I am particularly interested in knowing if you have obtained samples from the two mummified dogs excavated in 1916 by Guernsey and Kidder that I recently saw in the Peabody Museum (Peabody catalogue #s 16-9-10/A2994 and 16-9-10/A2997). Olsen (1985) describes one of these dogs as “black and white, about the size of a terrier, with a short haired but shaggy coat; the ears are erect, the tail long and bushy, and the muzzle is noticeably shortened.” (The dog looked more brown and white to me but Guernsey and Kidder (1921) also described it as black and white)  I would be very curious how these dogs fit into your findings.
