                                                            .publisher-anchor-color(data-role='message-content')
                                                                .post-message(data-role='message', dir='auto')
                                                                    div
                                                                        p
                                                                            | In recently completing a monograph on the aboriginal dogs of California, I realized that the reasons for disappearance of many types of dogs of the Native Americans described in the historical and ethnographic records were very complex, and varied even within the boundaries of California, depending on whether military conquest involved eliminating dogs that could be sentinels, the desire of the missionaries to make certain groups dependent on the missions and to assume a sedentary existence (to accomplish which some missions poisoned dogs of nearby tribes), wholesale genocide of groups and their dogs in the gold country, discouragement and eventual elimination of ritual and dietary practices involving dogs, preferences of certain tribes for European hunting dogs, etc. etc.In expanding this inquiry to the entire New World, I have identified an increasing number of patterns to explain why dogs of various groups disappeared, sometimes even when human members of a tribe survived with their cultural and subsistence patterns largely intact much longer than was true of their dogs.  When I first encountered genetic studies indicating survival of only a limited number of Native American dog types, I was shocked, but after assembling more and more historical and anthropological data, I am rather more surprised when a study, such as yours, finds evidence of any survival at all. Thank you for the excellent research.
